els and regularization strategies. The paper also identifies several limitations. The focus on linear models is a significant limitation, and future work should extend the analysis to non-linear models. The assumptions about the eigenvalue structure of the source and target covariances could be further explored. The experiments are limited to synthetic data, and future work should include experiments on real-world datasets. The practical implications of the theoretical results could be further investigated, and the computational cost of applying the proposed methods in practice should be considered. Future research should explore the generalization of these findings to non-linear models, develop more robust methods for estimating the relevant parameters, conduct experiments on real-world datasets, and develop practical guidelines for choosing appropriate models and regularization strategies. In conclusion, this paper makes a valuable contribution to the field of OOD generalization by providing a theoretical analysis of linear models under distribution shifts, particularly those affecting the covariance structure. The paper's findings offer valuable insights into the behavior of OLS, ridge regression, and PCR under different types of distribution shifts and provide a foundation for future research in this area. While the paper has some limitations, it provides a solid starting point for understanding the complex problem of OOD generalization.}

